grams, patient affordability offerings, digital banking, life science software technology solutions, and integrated payment processing services for businesses, consumers, and government institutions in the United States. The company offers solutions for corporate rewards, prepaid gift cards, general-purpose reloadable debit cards, employee incentives, consumer rebates, donor compensation, clinical trials, healthcare reimbursement payments and pharmaceutical payment assistance, and demand deposit accounts accessible with a debit card and software solutions. It also operates a customer service center; and offers a communication suite, including mobile app, two-way SMS, text alerts, and cardholder web portal. It markets its prepaid card solutions under the Paysign brand name. The company serves companies and municipalities that require payment solutions for rewards, rebates, payment assistance, and other payments to their customers, employees, agents, and others. Paysign, Inc. was founded in 2001 and is headquartered in Henderson, Nevada.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Mark R. Newcomer | Co-Founder, President, CEO & Director | Age 60 | Compensation: $1.4M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 226 people. Revenue per employee of $363K ranks in the below the sector median among Technology peers, with revenue growing 26%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $227K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $19K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 4\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (7/10).","financial_summary":["Paysign, Inc. is a small-cap technology business with a $634M market cap on $82M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +29% over the past three years and +26% annually across the full decade, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder.","Returns on capital run at 3% against a 10% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests. Gross margins of 50% confirm genuine pricing power.","The business throws off $51M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 62 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 6.95\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.5\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA.","Shareholder yield is only 0.1%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 21% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is, and profit capture is only 0.4\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Altman Z in the grey zone \u2014 financial health warrants monitoring","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 5.8% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $6.1M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Technology sector lagging the market significantly, underperforming by 9% over 3 months","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Technology ETF trading well below its long-term trend","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Elevated sector volatility (52% annualized) adds uncertainty","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Cybersecurity liability and talent cost inflation \u2014 a single breach can erase years of goodwill, and the war for engin